INT. NIGHT BUS 47 - MOVING - NIGHT

Rain streaks the windows. Sodium lights slide past. The engine drones.

ASH, 17, headphones around their neck, sits in the back seat with their phone glowing in both hands. Across the aisle, BRI, 17, hoodie up, chews her thumbnail. Two rows ahead, TEO, 18, stares out at nothing.

Up front, the DRIVER hums along to the radio. A sleeping TEENAGER in a school jacket leans against the window.

Dead quiet, until Bri's phone BUZZES.

INSERT - PHONE SCREEN

"mom: where r u. answer me."

BACK TO SCENE

BRI
(whispering)
She's literally gonna kill me.

ASH
So answer.

BRI
And say what? "Hi Mom, I'm on a bus to a city I've never been to"?

TEO
(turning around)
You guys could keep it down.

ASH
Nobody asked you, Teo.

TEO
I'm just saying, the driver.

BRI
He can't hear us.

ASH
He has a radio.

TEO
He has ears.

BRI
Ash, did you tell him about the video?

ASH
No, I told no one. They told me.

TEO
Who told you what?

BRI
Stop, you two are being so loud.

ASH
We're not loud, you're loud.

TEO
You're both loud.

The bus brakes hard. Everyone lurches. The sleeping teenager wakes, looks around, falls back asleep.

This is the last time the three of them will be calm.

ASH
(softer)
Hey. I don't think we're on the route anymore.

BRI
What?

ASH
Look. That's the river. We should be going north.

Teo stands. The Driver's humming has stopped.

The radio dies. Only the rain.

The Driver's eyes find them in the rear-view mirror.

DRIVER
Sit down, kids.
